<div class="project-container card">
    <div class="project-title">
        <div>
            <a class="project-link" href="/cv">
                <h4>CV Playground</h4>
            </a>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="project-description">
        <p>A real-time computer vision playground running on a live webcam feed in the browser, built to actually work rather than just demo well.</p>
        <p>
            A few problems came up that were worth solving properly. Color detection initially drew a bounding box on every frame
            regardless of match quality, so instead of taking the largest region by pixel count, the implementation picks the contour
            with the highest fill density (contour area divided by bounding rect area). That selects the region where the target color
            is most uniformly packed, and a slider lets you set the minimum density floor so weak matches don't draw at all.
            Bounding boxes across all detection modes were jittery frame-to-frame, so exponential moving average smoothing
            was added to the box coordinates — O(1) per frame and it naturally weights recent detections higher than a fixed window would.
            Template matching was extended to search across multiple scales and rotation angles up to 90 degrees, expanding the
            rotated template canvas to avoid edge clipping, with the result drawn as a rotated polygon rather than an axis-aligned box.
            The original plan was YOLOv8 via ONNX Runtime, but the model shipped as opset 21 which no browser runtime supports,
            so it was swapped for COCO-SSD via TensorFlow.js, which runs on a WebGL backend and fires asynchronously so inference
            never blocks the video feed.
        </p>
    </div>
    <div class="project-tags">
        <div class="tags-label">
